/*!
    \example simple_service_client
    \title Simple Service Client
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \brief Calling a std_srvs/SetBool service from QML and handling the
    response as a JS promise.

    \c{Simple Service Client} is the calling side of the \l{Simple Service}
    example: it wraps one \l{Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}{SetBoolServiceClient}
    in a \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{Node} and calls a \c std_srvs/SetBool
    service when you press \e{Turn lamp on} or \e{Turn lamp off}.

    \image simple_service_client/simple_service_client.png "The Simple Service Client window: an editable topic, on/off buttons, and the last response."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment, then launch Qt Creator from the same
            shell so it can find the generated QML modules.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_service_client/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator,
            then build and run.
        \li Provide the service by running the \l{Simple Service} example with
            the same topic. The buttons enable as soon as
            \c isServiceReady reports that the server was discovered.
    \endlist

    \section1 Calling a Service

    A service client is declared as a child of a \c Node. \c callService()
    returns a JavaScript promise that resolves with the response value — for
    \c std_srvs/SetBool an object carrying \c success and \c message:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.StdSrvs

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_service_client_node"

        SetBoolServiceClient {
            id: lampClient
            topic: topicField.text
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \qml
    lampClient.callService(true).then(response => {
        console.log(response.success, response.message)
    }, error => {
        console.log("call failed:", error)
    })
    \endqml

    A few things worth noting:

    \list
        \li \c isServiceReady is \c true while a server for \l topic is
            discovered on the ROS 2 graph — the example keeps its buttons
            disabled until then, so a call can never be attempted into the
            void.
        \li \c isCallPending is \c true from \c callService() until the
            response (or an error) arrives; the example uses it to prevent
            overlapping calls from button mashing.
        \li The promise rejects with an error string when the call fails, for
            example when the server disappears mid-call.
    \endlist

    \sa {Simple Service}, {Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}
*/
